course: dictionary
lesson: 3760
title: Flytrap: a word with two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning. The word flytrap is a good example. Its main meaning is a plant, the Venus flytrap, whose leaves snap shut like jaws to catch flies and other insects. «A Venus flytrap shuts in a fraction of a second» tells you the plant moves fast, which is a clue that it is a living thing with a trap, not an ordinary plant.
explain: A flytrap can also be a device that catches flies, such as a sticky strip. Look at the words around the word to choose the right meaning. If the sentence talks about leaves, trigger hairs or soil, it means the plant. If it talks about a sticky strip hanging in a kitchen, it means the device.
explain: The science lessons tell you more. A Venus flytrap is a carnivorous plant: it grows in poor, boggy soil, so it gets extra nutrients from insects. «Venus flytraps grow wild only in one small area of North and South Carolina in the USA» is a fact about where it lives, not a hint about what it eats.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. «The flytrap closed on the fly» only makes sense with the plant meaning. Always ask: does my meaning still make sense in the sentence?
fact: A Venus flytrap is a plant whose leaves snap shut like jaws to catch flies and other insects.
fact: The flytrap then digests the insects it catches, which gives it extra nutrients.
fact: A flytrap can also mean any device that catches flies, like a sticky strip.
fact: A Venus flytrap closes only if its trigger hairs are touched twice within about 20 seconds; one touch is not enough.
fact: Jokingly, flytrap can mean a person's open mouth, but this is slang, not science.
exercise: What is a Venus flytrap? | A tall tree that drops its seeds in autumn | A bird that eats seeds from the ground | *A plant whose leaves snap shut to catch flies and insects
exercise: «Venus flytraps grow wild only in one small area of North and South Carolina in the USA.» Where do they grow wild? | In every garden centre | Across all of Europe | *North and South Carolina in the USA
exercise: The soil where a flytrap grows is poor and boggy. Why does the plant catch insects? | It needs insects to water its roots | *Poor soil does not give it enough nutrients | Insects make the soil warmer
test: «A Venus flytrap closes only if its trigger hairs are touched twice within about 20 seconds.» How many touches make it close? | Ten | *Two | One
test: Someone jokingly calls a person's open mouth a flytrap. In this sentence, what does flytrap mean? | A plant that catches insects | *A mouth that opens wide, like a trap | A sticky strip for flies
exam: Using the science context, what does carnivorous plant mean? | *A plant that gets extra food by catching and digesting insects | A plant that grows in dry sand | A plant that only grows in the sea | A plant that eats only sunlight
